WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.600
is willing to pay for the revenue growth
and the maturation or maturing of a
company going from a loss-making operation
at the earnings level to a profit-making
operation.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:29.400
And this is why, for example, moving
forward with our price target estimates,
we've decided to utilize both earnings and
sales in order to come up with two price
targets,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:36.200
an upper bound bullish scenario, and a
lower bound realistic scenario.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:49.100
So what we're essentially saying here is
that if Zendesk going forward into 2020
hits its sales numbers, but doesn't quite
hit its earnings growth numbers,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:55.800
we should be expecting the stock to
probably be around $100, $105.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:04.600
But if they deliver and they really hit
those earnings numbers, so the $0.57 for
the full year 2020,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:13.400
we could see this stock hit $130 to $140
by the end of 2020.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.200
Now, it's important to note that there is
no one way of doing this coming up with
price targets.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:28.200
It will change on a case-by-case basis
across different sectors and across
different stocks.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:34.200
But you just have to stick to a couple of
principles here.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:43.000
Firstly, you're going to be utilizing both
earnings and sales estimates and keeping
valuation constant,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.000
utilizing reasonable assumptions.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
And secondly, you're going to come up with
a range.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.800
You're going to come up with a very
conservative number.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.800
And you're also going to come up with a
blue sky number.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.800
And as mentioned previously, this is not
an exact science.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:09.600
And of course, there is not a rule book, a
one-size-fits-all that you can apply to
all situations,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.600
across all stocks, across all sectors.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.600
There is discretion.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.600
There is common sense.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.600
And you just have to use reasonable
assumptions and be intellectually honest
with yourself.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:33.400
So overall, when it comes to the trade
idea generation template and the first
section, the quantitative analysis,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:42.400
what you're trying to achieve here is
obviously getting the numbers right, as
accurate as possible in your spreadsheets

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.400
before you copy the numbers over into the
Word document.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:53.400
And you want to use reasonable assumptions
to come to price targets.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:03.400
So you can arrive at sensible and
reasonable conclusions as to where you
think the stock could go in the next
period.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.400
And of course, you're going to document
this in the Word document

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.400
and literally spell out what type of trade
it is.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:20.800
At any one time, you're probably going to
be managing multiple trade idea generation
templates.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:29.200
So when you open these up, you need to be
reminded very quickly with bullet points
what the exact situation is.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.600
Now, what we can do here with our example
with Zendesk

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:45.400
is show you how things would have worked
out if you'd have actually done your work
on Zendesk and decided to buy it in May
2020.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:51.800
And we can show you how things actually
will have worked out over periods of our
time horizon.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.000
So our 20 to 60 day time horizon.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:06.800
So if you recall, we were basing our
quantitative work off the stock price at
the end of 2019, which was $76.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.800
And the stock price in May 2020 was
actually $76 as well.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:22.200
And what we've denoted here on the chart
is with the red arrows, we've denoted
periods of 60 days.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:36.200
So if you'd have bought the stock in May
2020, we fast forward to 60 days later and
have a look where the stock was at the end
of that period of 60 days.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.000
This is in August 2020.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.600
And then we assume another 60 days going
forward to November 2020.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:53.000
And then another 60 days going forward to
around the beginning of February 2021.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.400
And then in yellow for our yellow arrows,
we've denoted our price targets.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:12.200
So if you remember, we had price targets
in 2020 for $102 and 60 cents and $136 and
80 cents.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:25.000
And in 2021, if you refer back to the
table, you'll see that we actually had a
third price target for $166 and 69 cents.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:37.600
So the arrows, what the chart is showing
you here is with reference to the price
targets when the stock actually hit those
price targets.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:50.400
So as you're aware, the quantitative
analysis, for the example, Zendesk at the
top of the template that you're looking at
was taken in May 2020.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:06:02.400
And the chart that we just looked at, the
Zen chart shows this date and what would
have happened if you went long Zendesk for
60 day periods.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:14.400
And yes, the other parts of the trade idea
template that you're looking at as the
example, they were filled in also in May
2020.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.200
So we will be looking at those other parts
of the trade idea template soon.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:30.080
But let's run through the subsequent stock
price movement and when this stock Zendesk
actually hit our price targets.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:58.640
So if you'd have bought Zendesk in May
2020, our first price target, price target
one, our conservative estimate of $102 and
60 cents, which was based on a 2020 sales
forecast and utilizing the 2019 sales
multiple as a constant that was actually
reached within four and a half months.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:05.640
So it definitely hit the price in October
2020.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:15.600
However, if you look back at the chart,
you'll see that it basically traded at or
around that price at the end of July.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:36.480
So it's very likely if you had that trade
on that if the stock hit around $101, $102
because your price target was so close,
you probably would have been into that
trade and out of that trade within the 60
day time horizon.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:37.600
But let's say that you didn't trade out.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.600
But let's say that you didn't trade out.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:57.600
Well, the stock actually came off and then
it went back to above 100 in October and
it would have taken around four and a half
months for the stock to hit $102, $102.60
price target.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:04.600
So if we're using 20 trading days in a
month, that would have been around 90
trading days to reach the price target.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:19.600
But let's say you did actually trade out
at around $100, $101, $102 either at the
end of July or the end of September,
beginning of October.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:26.600
If you're in around 76, you're looking at
around $25 a share profit.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:37.600
So you're looking at a profit percentage
wise of around 30, 33%, something like
this over the time horizon.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:50.600
Now, if you went into the stock in May and
you decided to just get out at 60 days,
it's likely that you would have traded out
around that time period.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:55.600
It's likely that you will have traded out
around the mid 80s.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.600
So let's assume you made $10 a share.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:08.600
You're looking at about a 13% return
roughly from a starting price of, say, 75,
76.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:18.600
So you're looking at, say, a 13, 14, 15%
return if you just waited and traded out
at exactly 60 days.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:24.600
So this kind of shows you this example
here based on price targets.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:31.600
That's being active in your trading and
not holding on for the last dollar or the
last tick.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:38.600
And taking a probabilistic approach is
just a sensible thing to do.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:45.600
If you take a position in a stock, you're
operating in the 20 to 60 day time
horizon.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:57.600
And 30, 40 trading days goes by and the
stock is trading at or around your price
target.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:04.600
It's just a sensible thing to do to trade
on a probabilistic scenario.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:13.600
But if you're taking some sort of a
formulaic approach where you're just going
to go into stocks and trade out on exactly
day 60,

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:19.600
the stock is probably not going to be at
your price target.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:25.600
So when you've got the chance after 30, 40
days has gone by,

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:32.600
if the stock is at or around your price
target, you're probably trading out.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:46.600
And then, of course, you can sit back and
wait for a while and reassess the
situation based on new fundamentals that
are current in the market at the time.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:52.600
And yes, of course, you can go back into a
position, back into a stock.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:11:04.600
But you have to reassess the new
fundamentals again at the time and assess
whether you would actually buy the stock
at new prices.

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:08.600
And of course, there's always opportunity
cost of capital.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:16.600
So given that the stock is now moved, what
does the idea look like relative to other
ideas that you have?

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:20.600
And is it actually better than those
ideas?

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:34.600
Now, with our Zendesk example, let's
assume that you either sat back and waited
after doing your work in May or you took a
position and traded out.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:43.600
Either way, let's just assume that you got
into this stock in the mid 80s in August.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.600
Our price target one of $102.60.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:55.600
If you'd have got in in August, as we
know, based on our sales forecasts and
sales multiple analysis,

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:59.600
that was reached at the beginning of
October 2020.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:16.600
But our second price target, our price
target two of $136.80, which was based on
our earnings analysis and a continuation
of the forward multiple that was reached
in December 2020.

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:21.600
So that was seven months after the May
analysis.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:36.600
But if you'd have got into the stock in
mid August, around the mid 80s share
price, you will have seen price target
one, the $102.60.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:40.600
You will have seen that reached in the
first week of October.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:49.600
And price target two reached actually
outside, just outside the 60 day time
horizon.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:59.600
So the $136.80 was reached in the first
week of December.

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:04.600
So that was a couple of weeks after the 60
day time horizon closing.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:20.600
So given the two examples that we just
looked at here, where we're going into the
stock in May or going into the stock in
August, you can see that the 20 to 60 day
time horizon, it's more of a guide than an
exact science.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:35.600
You're always going into a stock with the
expectation that you wanted to move within
the 20 to 60 day time horizon and have the
volatility available for that to happen.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:40.600
However, you're also acknowledging that
you're going to be trading on a
probabilistic basis.

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:54.600
So in some situations, your price targets
or very close to your price targets, that
outcome is going to occur within or even
well within your time horizon.

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:58.600
And on a probabilistic scenario, you're
going to trade out.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:12.600
And then in other situations, you're going
to get the outcome where your price target
one is reached and that it's looking
highly likely that the stock could even go
further on and reach price target two.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:18.600
If you just hold on for a little while
beyond the 60 day time horizon.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:19.600
And that's OK, too.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.600
The point is here, guys, it's not an exact
science.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.600
It's a scientific art.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:36.600
Now, what's interesting about the Zendesk
example, the stock actually reached one
hundred and sixty dollars in nine months.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:52.600
So in February 2021 and then afterwards,
it fell back from that level to around the
forward multiple price target of around
one hundred and thirty six dollars in
March 2021.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:59.600
So what's interesting here, our price
targets of just over one hundred and sixty
six dollars.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:04.600
The stock almost got there in very early
2021.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:11.600
But that one hundred and sixty six dollar
price target that we had was for the end
of 2021.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:22.600
So we could argue there sitting in
February 2021 that potentially that the
stock price went too far too quick.

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:28.600
And the fundamentals would have had to
catch up with price.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:29.600
And what happened?

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:34.600
Well, it was too far too quick and price
simply corrected.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:48.600
So we chose Zendesk as an example here as
it was a pretty good long idea, a solid
long idea in 2020 at the time that ticked
many boxes.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:58.600
But it serves as a good example as a
revenue growth story maturing into
profitability in 2021.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:00.600
So use this case study.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:16.600
Use this example moving forward when
you're doing your quantitative analysis
and filling out the trade idea template
and making sure obviously that your tables
are as accurate as possible in your
spreadsheets.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:31.600
Then moving them over to the word document
documenting what type of profile the trade
actually is and then establishing your
price targets based on reasonable
assumptions.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:37.600
So now we're actually going to look at
other areas of the trade idea template.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:48.600
And just as a side note, we've purposely
kept blank certain parts of the template
for the Zendesk example.

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:57.600
These are sections, of course, going
forward for all of your future trade ideas
that you're going to be filling in
yourself.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:04.600
But what I am going to do is obviously
talk you through those areas and some best
practices.

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:17.600
So in the next section of the trade idea
generation template, you're going to be
documenting all of the sector quant.

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:29.600
So if you look at the table here, you'll
see Zendesk at the bottom of the table
underneath the row for averages.

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:34.600
And then above that, you'll have each row
for each stock.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:42.600
And you'll see in the table here, we've
got columns for PE1, EG1, EG2.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.600
We got PEG1 and market cap.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:49.600
And of course, you can add some more
columns in there as well if you want to.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:52.600
This is just a template, obviously.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:57.600
So in this part of the template, of
course, we want to get our numbers right
again.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:06.600
And we want to display visually the
identification of the trade on a
quantitative basis versus the sector.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:09.600
So we want to be looking at this visually.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:19.600
And it's very, very obvious that we have a
positive or negative outlier or it's a
turnaround or value trap.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:38.600
So this table will essentially be a copy
and paste from your spreadsheets with all
of your sector work and the stock
quantitative relative to the sector
averages with all of the stocks in the
sector that are contributing to that
average.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:49.600
So it's very easy to see visually the
quantitative numbers of the stock relative
to everything in the sector.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:19:04.600
And you'll notice in the template that
underneath the sector data table for
quantitative, we've got some comments in a
text box on the quantitative analysis
sector comparison.

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:15.600
And the first thing you're going to do is
you're going to document whether the stock
is a quantitative outlier or not in that
text box.

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:23.600
So as you must recall now, we've obviously
been through the whole quantitative
process earlier in the video series.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:34.600
This is where you're basically putting
your accurate sector quantitative work and
you're literally spelling out what type of
trade this is.

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:46.600
So whether it's a positive or negative
outlier or perhaps a turnaround value trap
or even very specifically on a short, an X
growth stock in phase two.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:51.600
Now, some other stuff that you would put
into this text box.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:20:02.600
You're going to put in there sector
drivers, fundamental sector drivers that
could provide a tailwind or a headwind to
the sector fundamentally.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:12.600
So within the time horizon, 20 to 60 days,
things that could affect the entire sector
positively or negatively.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:24.600
So for example, that would be that would
be like the oil price for the oil sector
or microchip or DRAM prices for the tech
hardware sector.

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:31.600
So documenting all of this stuff in the
template is obviously good discipline.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:33.600
It's good process.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:46.600
And it all acts as a reminder as to why
you're in a stock and also what are the
tailwinds or headwinds fundamental
fundamentally at a sector level.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:55.600
So when you open up your templates, if
you've not looked at it for a few weeks,
you can see it all documented and get up
to speed very quickly.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:21:00.600
Also, what you'd put in this section is a
volatility appraisal.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:04.600
So you would look at realized and implied
volatility as well.

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:10.600
And you do a single stock volatility
appraisal versus the rest of the sector.

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:15.600
So this can be done in a number of ways,
as you're fully aware by now.

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:27.600
This can be done by looking at the implied
vols for the stock in the options prices
and inferring a monthly move or a
quarterly move.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:32.600
Or you can use options prices to calculate
it yourself.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:35.600
So getting implied volatility yourself.

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:39.600
And you can also do the work on realized
volatility as well.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:48.600
So you can utilize distribution of returns
analysis and ATR analysis that we've
covered in the IPLT video series.

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:53.600
And also some of that in the PTM video
series, too.

00:21:53.600 --> 00:22:03.600
So when you're doing your volatility
appraisal, what you're attempting to
understand is how volatile the stock
actually is compared to its peer group.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:14.600
And you're going to arrive at an
expectation of one to three month
volatility for the stock versus the
sector.

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:28.600
And then you're going to match that up
with your price target expectations to
make sure that your expectations are
actually realistic within the 20 to 60 day
time horizon.

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:38.600
And when you're going through this
process, you may actually have to adjust
your expectation for price targets for the
lower bound and the upper bound.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.600
So work out realized volatility of the
stock.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:46.600
Work out implied volatility for the stock.

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:53.600
Get an inferred one to three month
expected vol number for the stock itself.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:23:00.600
Then you can compare this if you do the
same analysis for other stocks in the
sector.

00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:11.600
You can compare this to those other stocks
to get an understanding of whether the
stock is volatile compared to other stocks
in the sector.

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:23.600
So you get a solid understanding of the
absolute volatility of the stock itself
and also the relative volatility of the
stock versus other stocks in the sector.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:32.600
And then you try to set your price target
expectations around those numbers if they
need to be adjusted.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:42.600
In terms of the template, you're going to
get all of this information in this
section of the template and be very
disciplined doing it,

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:51.600
making sure that you know exactly what
you're dealing with in terms of the
quantitative and therefore the overall
parameters of the trade idea.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:58.600
Next section of the template is the
qualitative section.

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:06.600
So we're going to be looking here in this
section at documenting the business itself
and business drivers.

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:19.600
So in this part of the template, we're
aiming to document the actual business,
what they actually do and the drivers of
the business that are behind revenue and
earnings.

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:29.600
So here we're documenting the key
performance indicators, KPIs to infer the
management operating plan.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:33.600
So we've put some example text in there
for Zendesk.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:43.600
Of course, if you're doing this for real
yourself, you will have probably done much
more work than what's there in the
template.

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:59.600
This is simply an example of the process
of how you should go about things and in
the actual order that you should go
through the processes to take a formalized
uniform approach.

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:06.600
So in the first box, you're going to
succinctly explain exactly what the
company does.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:11.600
If you can't say it in one or two
sentences, then you're probably confused.

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:23.600
And that's a very simple process where
it's obviously a bit more detailed and a
lot more work is going to be in the next
text area of the template, which is for
KPIs.

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:28.600
So in the next section, you're going to
fill in all of the main KPIs.

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:37.600
So the drivers behind revenue and
earnings, how the company measures the
success of its business.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:43.600
So its business drivers behind revenue and
earnings and forward looking expectations.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:55.600
So this box, once you've actually filled
it out, is essentially the management
operating plan documented and the
management targets.

